This issue was created from Discord post 1501234448664887469:

[![Open in Discord](https://img.shields.io/badge/Open_in_Discord-5865F2?style=for-the-badge&logo=discord&logoColor=white)](https://discord.com/channels/1309558646228779139/1501234448664887469)

Hi team! I have a working custom chat route that responds a steam that my client ingests with Vercel's ai-sdk. Now, I'm trying to implement durable agent so we'll have resumable streams but I'm noticing a change in behaviour and I'm not sure if I'm missing anything or if it's a valid issue.

- `data-om-status` is no longer saved in storage but is streamed to the client
- the last assistant message is not streamed to the client. This includes the `text`, `data-om-thread-update`, and `data-om-buffering-end` types -- needs a reload to fetch historical messages

Here is how the custom route is defined:

```
registerApiRoute('/chat/:agentId', {
method: 'POST',
handler: async c => {
const { messages, memory } = await c.req.json();
const mastra = c.get('mastra');
const agent = mastra.getAgentById(c.req.param('agentId'));
const durableAgent = new DurableAgent({ agent });

const {
cleanup,
runId,
output
} = await durableAgent.stream(messages, {
memory
});

void output.consumeStream().catch(error => {
mastra
.getLogger()
?.error('...', { error });
});

const uiStream = createUIMessageStream({
originalMessages: messages,
execute: async ({ writer }) => {
const reader = toAISdkStream(output, {
from: 'agent',
sendReasoning: true,
version: 'v6'
}).getReader();
let result = await reader.read();
while (!result.done) {
writer.write(result.value as InferUIMessageChunk<UIMessage>);
result = await reader.read();
}
reader.releaseLock();
}
// onFinish: () => cleanup() // TODO: review, ends the stream prematurely and causes errors
});

return createUIMessageStreamResponse({ stream: uiStream });
}
});
```

Some notes for above:
- this doesn't implement resuming any streams yet
- we're instantiating the durable agent within the route just to avoid strict Typescript lint errors
- still figuring out where to call `cleanup()` without affecting the stream

Additional context on the agent:
- OM is enabled
- with output processor
- save per step is enabled

## Summary

`@mastra/client-js` drops `providerMetadata` (e.g. `vertex.thoughtSignature`) when rebuilding the message stack for a recursive client-tool stream call. The metadata is placed inside the `toolInvocation` object instead of on the parent V2 part, so the server's `MessageList` adapters (which read it from `part.providerMetadata`) never see it on the next turn.

This was almost-but-not-quite fixed by #16078. That PR added the namespace merge in `MessageList.updateToolInvocation()` server-side, but the merge can only preserve metadata that arrives at `part.providerMetadata` — and the client SDK puts it elsewhere. So for any flow that goes through a `clientTool` continuation (the SDK's `agent.stream(..., { clientTools })` path), the signature is still lost.

## Impact

With Gemini thinking models (`gemini-3-flash-preview`, `gemini-3-pro-preview`, etc.) the recursive tool-result continuation fails:

```
AI_APICallError: Function call is missing a thought_signature in functionCall parts.
This is required for tools to work correctly...
```

Any `agent.stream()` call that uses `clientTools` against a Gemini thinking model fails on the second turn.

## Reproduction

```ts
import { MastraClient, createTool } from '@mastra/client-js';
import { z } from 'zod';

const client = new MastraClient({ baseUrl: 'http://127.0.0.1:4111' });
const agent = client.getAgent('myAgent'); // agent must use a Gemini thinking model

const res = await agent.stream(
  [{ role: 'user', content: 'Call presentList with kind=link items=[{href:"https://a",label:"A"}]' }],
  {
    memory: { thread: { id: crypto.randomUUID() }, resource: 'user-1' },
    clientTools: {
      presentList: createTool({
        id: 'presentList',
        description: 'Render a list',
        inputSchema: z.object({ kind: z.string(), items: z.array(z.unknown()) }),
        execute: async () => ({ ok: true }),
      }),
    },
  },
);
await res.processDataStream({ onChunk: c => console.log(c.type) });
// Stream emits a `tool-call` with providerMetadata, FE execute returns,
// recursion POSTs back without providerMetadata at part level → 400 from Gemini.
```

## Root cause

`processChatResponse_vNext` in `client-sdks/client-js/src/resources/agent.ts`:

```ts
const invocation = {
  state: 'call',
  step,
  ...chunk.payload,   // includes providerMetadata
} as const;

updateToolInvocationPart(chunk.payload.toolCallId, invocation);
// → part = { type: 'tool-invocation', toolInvocation: { ..., providerMetadata } }
```

But the server-side `MessageList` adapter expects `providerMetadata` as a sibling of `toolInvocation` on the part itself (`packages/core/src/agent/message-list/message-list.ts:736`).

## Fix

Route `providerMetadata` from the chunk payload to `part.providerMetadata` in `updateToolInvocationPart`. PR: #16218.

## Environment

- `@mastra/core@1.32.1`
- `@mastra/client-js@1.17.1`
- Provider: `@ai-sdk/google` with `gemini-3-flash-preview`
- Node 22

## Bug

`PostgresStore.saveThread` uses `ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET metadata = EXCLUDED.metadata` — a full replacement. `#executeOnFinish` calls `memory.createThread({ metadata: thread.metadata })` where `thread.metadata` is the stale in-memory object (often `undefined`), which overwrites any metadata written during the run by processors via `updateThread`.

`updateThread` already merges metadata correctly. `saveThread` should do the same.

## Why `#executeOnFinish` triggers a second save

In `prepare-memory-step.ts`, when a thread is new:

1. `existingThread` is `null`
2. `memory.createThread({ saveThread: true })` creates the thread in DB ([line 155](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/main/packages/core/src/agent/workflows/prepare-stream/prepare-memory-step.ts#L155))
3. But `threadExists` is set to `!!existingThread` — still `false` ([line 194](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/main/packages/core/src/agent/workflows/prepare-stream/prepare-memory-step.ts#L194))

This `false` propagates to `#executeOnFinish` via `map-results-step.ts` ([line 302](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/main/packages/core/src/agent/workflows/prepare-stream/map-results-step.ts#L302)), which calls `memory.createThread()` again ([line 5378](https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/main/packages/core/src/agent/agent.ts#L5378)) — triggering the upsert that replaces metadata.

## Reproduction

```typescript
const memory = new Memory({ storage: new PostgresStore({ connectionString }) });

// 1. Create thread (simulating prepare-memory-step.ts creating the thread)
await memory.saveThread({ thread: { id: "t1", resourceId: "r1", title: "", metadata: undefined, createdAt: new Date(), updatedAt: new Date() } });

// 2. Write metadata via updateThread (simulating a processor writing metadata mid-run)
const store = await memory.storage.getStore("memory");
await store.updateThread({ id: "t1", title: "", metadata: { myKey: "myValue" } });

// 3. saveThread again with undefined metadata (simulating #executeOnFinish with threadExists=false)
await memory.saveThread({ thread: { id: "t1", resourceId: "r1", title: "", metadata: undefined, createdAt: new Date(), updatedAt: new Date() } });

// 4. Check — myKey is gone
const thread = await store.getThreadById({ threadId: "t1" });
console.log(thread.metadata); // null — expected: { myKey: "myValue" }
```

Also reproduces with LibSQLStore — not PostgresStore-specific.

## Environment

- `@mastra/core`: 1.28.0
- `@mastra/memory`: 1.17.1
- `@mastra/pg`: 1.9.0

