This issue was created from Discord post 1500630029179027577:

[![Open in Discord](https://img.shields.io/badge/Open_in_Discord-5865F2?style=for-the-badge&logo=discord&logoColor=white)](https://discord.com/channels/1309558646228779139/1500630029179027577)

@mastra/core: workflow abort listener retention
We found completed workflow executions retained in heap snapshots through abort listener closures in nested workflow execution.

In Workflow.execute, nestedAbortCb closes over the run object and is registered on run.abortController.signal. A parent abort listener is also registered on the caller-provided abortSignal. After successful completion, these listeners were not removed, leaving completed Run instances reachable.

Observed heap path included Function closure nestedAbortCb retaining the Run, which then retained request/workflow state.

Patch shape:

```const parentAbortCb = async () => {
  run.abortController.signal.removeEventListener("abort", nestedAbortCb);
  await run.cancel();
};
run.abortController.signal.addEventListener("abort", nestedAbortCb);
abortSignal.addEventListener("abort", parentAbortCb);
try {
  // existing timeTravel/restart/resume/start execution
} finally {
  run.abortController.signal.removeEventListener("abort", nestedAbortCb);
  abortSignal.removeEventListener("abort", parentAbortCb);
  unwatch();
}```
Validation: after repeated assistant HTTP runs and forced-GC heap snapshots, retained Run instances dropped to 0.

## Summary

Harness display state currently tracks live subagent executions through `activeSubagents`, but it does not retain completed/error/aborted subagent activity in the terminal display snapshot.

This is a feature/design gap, not a bug in `activeSubagents`.

`activeSubagents` can remain live/current state. The missing primitive is a durable display-state field, such as `subagentHistory` or `subagentTimeline`, that survives `agent_end` so UIs, reconnect flows, and display adapters can render completed delegated work without reconstructing Harness semantics from raw events.

## Current behavior

Verified against current main:

- `HarnessDisplayState` exposes `activeSubagents: Map<string, ActiveSubagentState>`, but no completed/history/timeline field.
- `subagent_end` marks the active subagent as `completed` or `error`.
- `agent_end` later clears all subagents with `ds.activeSubagents = new Map()`.
- The current test suite explicitly expects `activeSubagents` to be empty after `agent_end`.

So a UI subscribed to display state can briefly observe a completed subagent after `subagent_end`, but the latest terminal display snapshot after `agent_end` no longer contains that completed subagent activity.

## Why this matters

Mastra docs position Harness display state as the UI-facing snapshot:

- `getDisplayState()` returns the current `HarnessDisplayState` for rendering.
- `subscribeDisplayState()` is the recommended path for UI/SSE/TUI/bridge consumers that only need the latest display state.
- Raw `subscribe()` events are better suited for logs, debugging, audit trails, and deterministic replay.

That means display state should contain enough information for the latest UI snapshot. For delegated agent workflows, completed subagent activity is part of what the user needs to see.

A production UI often needs to show:

- which subagents were invoked
- whether each subagent completed, errored, or was aborted
- useful terminal details such as duration/result summary
- a stable final activity view after the parent run finishes
- the same completed delegation state after reconnect or replay

Today, apps that need this must maintain their own completed-subagent ledger outside Harness and merge it back into terminal UI state.

## Existing asymmetry

Harness already preserves completed tool activity in final display state.

On `agent_end`, still-running tools are marked as `error`, while completed tools remain available in `activeTools`. Subagents are different: they are marked completed/error on `subagent_end`, but then all subagent state is cleared on `agent_end`.

That means final UI can still render completed tool activity, but loses completed delegated subagent activity.

## Proposed behavior

Keep `activeSubagents` semantics unchanged.

Add a separate durable display-state field, for example:

```ts
export interface HarnessSubagentHistoryEntry extends ActiveSubagentState {
  toolCallId: string;
  endedAt?: Date;
  order?: number;
  parentEndReason?: 'complete' | 'aborted' | 'error' | 'suspended';
}

export interface HarnessDisplayState {
  activeSubagents: Map<string, ActiveSubagentState>;
  subagentHistory: HarnessSubagentHistoryEntry[];
}
```

The exact name could be `completedSubagents`, `subagentHistory`, or `subagentTimeline`, but `subagentHistory` seems like the best fit because it can include completed, errored, and aborted entries without implying raw event-level replay.

## Suggested semantics

- `activeSubagents` remains the live/current subagent map.
- `subagent_start` adds/updates `activeSubagents`.
- `subagent_end` records the final subagent state into `subagentHistory`.
- `agent_end` clears `activeSubagents`, but does not erase `subagentHistory`.
- If `agent_end` occurs while subagents are still running, Harness should copy those entries into `subagentHistory` with a terminal status such as `error` or `aborted`.
- `subagentHistory` should reset on new display-state boundaries such as `agent_start`, `thread_changed`, `thread_created`, and `thread_deleted`, unless Harness intentionally wants whole-thread subagent history.

## Suggested tests

Add focused display-state tests covering:

```ts
// During run
expect(displayState.activeSubagents.size).toBe(1);

// After subagent_end
expect(displayState.subagentHistory).toContainEqual(
  expect.objectContaining({
    toolCallId: 's1',
    status: 'completed',
    durationMs: 1234,
  }),
);

// After agent_end
expect(displayState.activeSubagents.size).toBe(0);
expect(displayState.subagentHistory).toHaveLength(1);

// Missing subagent_end / aborted parent run
expect(displayState.subagentHistory[0]).toEqual(
  expect.objectContaining({
    toolCallId: 's1',
    status: 'aborted', // or 'error'
    parentEndReason: 'aborted',
  }),
);
```

## Related but distinct from #15975

This is related to #15975, which asks for a Harness-native AI SDK UI display stream adapter.

However, this issue is lower-level. A Harness-to-AI-SDK adapter can only faithfully render terminal/reconnect state if `HarnessDisplayState` itself retains completed subagent activity. Otherwise every adapter still has to reconstruct subagent history from raw events.

This issue asks for the Harness display-state model to retain enough delegated subagent state for terminal UI snapshots, reconnect, and display adapters.

## Bug

`ToolCallFilter` can drop an assistant message that still has valid top-level text content.

When a message uses v2 content with:

- `content.content` containing assistant text, and
- `content.parts` containing only filtered `tool-invocation` parts,

the processor removes all parts and then returns `null` for the whole message. The top-level text fallback is lost.

## Expected behavior

Filtering tool invocation parts should not remove a message that still has non-tool text content.

For example, after filtering tool history, this message should survive with its text:

```ts
{
  role: 'assistant',
  content: {
    format: 2,
    content: 'I found three relevant papers.',
    parts: [
      {
        type: 'tool-invocation',
        toolInvocation: {
          state: 'result',
          toolCallId: 'call-1',
          toolName: 'search_papers',
          args: { query: 'attention mechanisms' },
          result: { papers: ['paper-1', 'paper-2', 'paper-3'] },
        },
      },
    ],
  },
}
```

## Current behavior

The message is removed entirely.

A focused regression test against `ToolCallFilter.processInput()` currently fails with:

```text
AssertionError: expected [] to have a length of 1 but got +0
```

## Why this happens

Verified against latest `origin/main` at commit:

```text
9efa13f3575d681f3231087de80c480b7408d64f
```

In `packages/core/src/processors/processors/tool-call-filter.ts`, both filtering paths drop the message when the filtered parts array is empty:

- `filterAllToolCalls()` returns `null` when `nonToolParts.length === 0`: https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/9efa13f3575d681f3231087de80c480b7408d64f/packages/core/src/processors/processors/tool-call-filter.ts#L145-L157
- `filterSpecificToolCalls()` returns `null` when `filteredParts.length === 0`: https://github.com/mastra-ai/mastra/blob/9efa13f3575d681f3231087de80c480b7408d64f/packages/core/src/processors/processors/tool-call-filter.ts#L214-L242

Those checks only consider `parts`, not the top-level `content.content` text that is later spread into `updatedContent`.

## Minimal reproduction shape

A focused test can be added near `packages/core/src/processors/processors/tool-call-filter.test.ts`:

```ts
