# Bug: `@mastra/schema-compat`'s `JsonSchemaWrapper` is unusable on Cloudflare Workers (workerd)

**Affected packages**
- `@mastra/schema-compat@1.2.6` — root cause (uses AJV)
- `@mastra/mcp@1.2.1` — primary trigger (creates JSON-Schema-only tools on the client side)
- `@mastra/core@1.15.0` — calls `toStandardSchema` from the affected dispatcher

**Affected runtimes**: Cloudflare Workers (workerd), Vercel Edge Runtime, Deno Deploy, any V8 isolate that disables `eval` / `new Function` via Content Security Policy.

**Severity**: Tools sourced via `MCPClient.listToolsets()` and called from an agent fail at runtime with `Schema validation error: Code generation from strings disallowed for this context`. The MCP client path is effectively dead on workerd whenever the upstream MCP server publishes tools with non-trivial JSON Schema input.

---

## Symptom

When an agent running on workerd calls a tool whose definition came from `MCPClient.listToolsets()`, validation fails with this user-visible error returned to the LLM:

```
Tool input validation failed for searchGics. Please fix the following errors and try again:
- root: Schema validation error: Code generation from strings disallowed for this context

Provided arguments: {
  "query": "agriculture chemicals"
}
```

Preceded in logs by AJV codegen output:

```
Error compiling schema, function code: const schema2 = scope.schema[2]; const schema1 = scope.schema[1]; return function validate1(data, {instancePath="", parentData, parentDataProperty, rootData=data}={}){ ... }
```

The agent never reaches the actual MCP `tools/call` request — the failure is inside Mastra's own pre-call validation on the client side.

## Why workerd rejects it

workerd disallows runtime code evaluation:

- `eval(string)` → blocked
- `new Function(string, ...)` → blocked

This is enforced by the V8 isolate's CSP. AJV's default validator generation goes through `new Function(...)` (see `ajv/dist/compile/index.ts` → `makeValidate`) and is therefore unusable in workerd.

## Reproduction

```ts
import { MCPClient } from "@mastra/mcp";
import { Agent } from "@mastra/core/agent";

// Connect to any MCP server that publishes a tool with a non-trivial JSON Schema input.
const mcp = new MCPClient({ servers: { upstream: { url: "https://example.com/mcp" } } });
const toolsets = await mcp.listToolsets();

const agent = new Agent({ name: "repro", instructions: "...", model });
await agent.stream("call upstream.searchGics with query 'agriculture chemicals'", {
  toolsets,
});
```

Deployed to a Cloudflare Worker, this throws the symptom error.

## Root cause

`InternalMastraMCPClient` wraps each upstream MCP tool in a Mastra `Tool` via `createTool`, passing the *raw JSON Schema* through unchanged:

**File**: `@mastra/mcp@1.2.1/dist/index.js`, around line 924

```js
const mastraTool = createTool({
  id: `${this.name}_${tool.name}`,
  description: tool.description || "",
  inputSchema: await this.convertInputSchema(tool.inputSchema),    // ← raw JSON Schema
  outputSchema: await this.convertOutputSchema(tool.outputSchema), // ← raw JSON Schema
  execute: async (input, ctx) => this.client.callTool({ name: tool.name, arguments: input }, ...),
});
```

`createTool` then runs the schema through `toStandardSchema`:

**File**: `@mastra/core@1.15.0/dist/chunk-UPXPPD5L.js`, line 420

```js
this.inputSchema  = opts.inputSchema  ? toStandardSchema(opts.inputSchema)  : void 0;
this.outputSchema = opts.outputSchema ? toStandardSchema(opts.outputSchema) : void 0;
```

For a raw JSON Schema (not Zod, not Vercel-AI-SDK-shaped, not Standard Schema), `toStandardSchema`'s dispatcher falls through to `JsonSchemaWrapper`:

**File**: `@mastra/schema-compat@1.2.6/dist/chunk-QDRBTCDV.js`, lines 6303–6448

```js
#getAjvValidator() {
  if (!this.#ajvValidateCache) {
    this.#ajvInstance = new import_ajv.default({ allErrors: true, strict: false, ...this.#options.ajvOptions });
    this.#ajvValidateCache = this.#ajvInstance.compile(this.#schema); // ← new Function(...)
  }
  return this.#ajvValidateCache;
}
```

`AJV.compile(schema)` calls `new Function(...)`. workerd rejects this; the catch block surfaces it as a Standard Schema issue, which `validateToolInput` formats and returns to the LLM as the tool's error result.

## Security implications

This isn't only a workerd correctness bug — it's a trust-boundary smell on every runtime.

The schema flowing into `AJV.compile` originates from the upstream MCP server (a third-party process). Mastra's MCP client takes that attacker-controlled object and feeds it through codegen to `new Function`. That's the textbook shape of an RCE primitive: **untrusted data → codegen → eval**.

AJV's `Code` template-tag system is designed to escape interpolations and there's no known live CVE in current 8.x for direct schema-string-to-source injection. But:

- AJV has shipped prototype-pollution and parser-edge-case CVEs historically (e.g. CVE-2020-15366).
- `$ref` / `$id` resolution across schema validators has had repeated bypass bugs ecosystem-wide.
- The safety guarantee has to be re-proved on every AJV bump.

A fix that keeps untrusted JSON Schema *out of any codegen pipeline at all* — not just on workerd — is strictly better than one that swaps codegen libraries.

## Why the existing `jsonSchemaValidator` patch doesn't help

`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` exposes a `jsonSchemaValidator` option that swaps the SDK's internal AJV usage. The recent Mastra patch wires this through to the underlying SDK `Server` and `Client`. That covers SDK-internal validation. It does **not** cover `JsonSchemaWrapper`, which sits in `@mastra/schema-compat` and runs *before* the SDK Client ever sees the call. The AJV path described above is entirely inside Mastra and out of the SDK's reach.

---

## Proposed fix: convert MCP-sourced JSON Schemas to Zod at toolset-load time

The right layer to fix this is `@mastra/mcp`, not `@mastra/schema-compat`. AJV is the correct default for Mastra-native tools on Node — it's fast, well-audited, and supports `ajv-formats` / custom keywords. Swapping it out globally penalizes the 95% case.

But for **MCP-sourced** tools specifically, a JSON Schema is the only available representation and it's coming from outside the trust boundary. Convert that schema to a real Zod schema at `listToolsets()` time, replace the `inputSchema` / `outputSchema` on the tool, and downstream validation routes through Zod's native parser instead of AJV. This:

- Fixes workerd (no `new Function`)
- Closes the RCE gadget (no codegen on untrusted input, on any runtime)
- Leaves AJV in place as the default for non-MCP tools — Node hot path is untouched
- Pays the conversion cost once per toolset load, not per call
- Is a fully backwards-compatible additive change

Use [`zod-from-json-schema`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/zod-from-json-schema), which builds Zod schemas via the Zod API rather than emitting source. (Note: do **not** use `json-schema-to-zod`/`jsonSchemaToZod`, which emit source strings — those require `new Function` to instantiate and reproduce the original problem.)

### Userspace workaround (works today)

Until a first-party fix lands, MCP-on-workerd users can drop this in after `listToolsets()`:

```ts
import type { MCPClient } from "@mastra/mcp";
import { convertJsonSchemaToZod } from "zod-from-json-schema";

type StandardJsonSchemaShape = {
  "~standard"?: {
    jsonSchema?: {
      input?: (options: { target: string }) => Record<string, unknown>;
      output?: (options: { target: string }) => Record<string, unknown>;
    };
  };
};

interface MutableTool {
  id?: string;
  inputSchema?: StandardJsonSchemaShape;
  outputSchema?: StandardJsonSchemaShape;
}

export async function readMcpToolsets(client: MCPClient) {
  const toolsets = await client.listToolsets();
  coerceToolSchemasToZod(toolsets);
  return toolsets;
}

/**
 * Replaces each tool's JSON-Schema-backed `inputSchema` / `outputSchema` with
 * a Zod schema converted from the same JSON Schema. Mastra's default
 * `JsonSchemaWrapper` compiles the JSON Schema via Ajv at validation time,
 * which uses `new Function(...)` and is forbidden in workerd. Replacing with
 * a Zod schema routes validation through Zod's native parser instead.
 */
export function coerceToolSchemasToZod(toolsets: Record<string, Record<string, MutableTool>>): void {
  for (const serverName of Object.keys(toolsets)) {
    const tools = toolsets[serverName];
    for (const toolName of Object.keys(tools)) {
      const tool = tools[toolName];
      coerceFieldToZod(tool, "inputSchema", "input");
      coerceFieldToZod(tool, "outputSchema", "output");
    }
  }
}

function coerceFieldToZod(
  tool: MutableTool,
  field: "inputSchema" | "outputSchema",
  io: "input" | "output",
): void {
  const wrapper = tool[field];
  const jsonSchemaFn = wrapper?.["~standard"]?.jsonSchema?.[io];
  if (typeof jsonSchemaFn !== "function") return;
  const jsonSchema = jsonSchemaFn({ target: "draft-07" });
  tool[field] = convertJsonSchemaToZod(jsonSchema) as StandardJsonSchemaShape;
}
```

This works because `JsonSchemaWrapper` already exposes the raw JSON Schema via `~standard.jsonSchema.input/output`. The fix yanks it back out, builds a real Zod schema from it, and overwrites the wrapper before the agent ever sees the tool.

### First-party proposal

Land this as built-in behavior in `@mastra/mcp`:

**Option 1 — opt-in flag on `MCPClient`** (recommended; backwards-compatible):

```ts
const mcp = new MCPClient({
  servers: { upstream: { url: "..." } },
  coerceSchemasTo: "zod", // default: "json-schema" (current behavior)
});
```

When set to `"zod"`, `InternalMastraMCPClient.convertInputSchema` / `convertOutputSchema` runs the dereferenced JSON Schema through `zod-from-json-schema` and returns a Zod schema. `createTool` then takes the existing Zod path in `toStandardSchema` and AJV is never invoked for MCP tools.

**Option 2 — exported utility** for users who want explicit control:

```ts
import { MCPClient, coerceMcpToolsetsToZod } from "@mastra/mcp";
const toolsets = coerceMcpToolsetsToZod(await mcp.listToolsets());
```

Either works; Option 1 is the better default-on path so the next workerd user doesn't hit this.

### Why not "swap AJV for `@cfworker/json-schema` in `JsonSchemaWrapper`"

An earlier version of this issue proposed that. It was wrong:

- AJV is ~30–50× faster than interpreter-based validators on Node — that's the primary runtime.
- `ajv-formats` (email/uri/date-time/etc.), `ajv-keywords`, discriminator, custom keywords — all gone.
- It "fixes" the symptom for everyone but punishes the 95% case to do it.

Coercing only the MCP path leaves AJV doing what it does well (Mastra-native tools on Node) and only changes behavior where a JSON Schema is *forced* on the system from outside.

---

## Test plan

### Unit tests in `@mastra/mcp`

```ts
describe("coerceMcpToolsetsToZod", () => {
  it("replaces JsonSchemaWrapper-backed inputSchema with a Zod schema", async () => {
    const toolsets = await mcp.listToolsets(); // tool with JSON Schema input
    coerceMcpToolsetsToZod(toolsets);
    const tool = toolsets.upstream.upstream_echo;
    // The schema should now be a Zod schema, not a JsonSchemaWrapper.
    expect((tool.inputSchema as any)._def).toBeDefined();
  });

  it("validate path does not invoke `new Function`", async () => {
    const toolsets = await mcp.listToolsets();
    coerceMcpToolsetsToZod(toolsets);
    const fnSpy = vi.spyOn(globalThis, "Function");
    const tool = toolsets.upstream.upstream_echo;
    await tool.execute!({ message: "hi" }, {} as never);
    expect(fnSpy).not.toHaveBeenCalledWith(expect.any(String));
    fnSpy.mockRestore();
  });

  it("invalid input is rejected with a Zod-shaped issue", async () => {
    const toolsets = await mcp.listToolsets();
    coerceMcpToolsetsToZod(toolsets);
    const tool = toolsets.upstream.upstream_echo;
    const result = await tool.execute!({ message: 42 } as never, {} as never);
    // Should surface a Zod validation error, not an AJV error string.
    expect(result).toMatchObject({ error: expect.stringContaining("expected string") });
  });
});
```

### Integration test (workerd)

Add a workerd-runtime test using `@cloudflare/vitest-pool-workers`:

```ts
it("MCPClient with coerceSchemasTo: 'zod' produces a callable tool that validates without `new Function`", async () => {
  const mcp = new MCPClient({
    servers: { up: createInProcessMcpServer({ tools: [{ name: "echo", ... }] }) },
    coerceSchemasTo: "zod",
  });
  const toolsets = await mcp.listToolsets();
  const tool = toolsets.up.up_echo;
  const result = await tool.execute!({ message: "hi" }, {} as never);
  expect(result).toBeDefined();
});
```

This test fails today on the workerd pool with `Code generation from strings disallowed for this context` and should pass after the fix.

### End-to-end

Deploy a Cloudflare Worker that uses `MCPClient` with `coerceSchemasTo: "zod"` against any public MCP server. Have an agent call a tool whose input has a non-trivial JSON Schema. Today: 500. After fix: success.

### Regression coverage

- Mastra-native tools created with Zod / Vercel-AI-SDK / raw JSON Schema continue to work unchanged (AJV path preserved).
- The `coerceSchemasTo: "json-schema"` default produces byte-for-byte identical behavior to today.

---

## File-level checklist

1. **`@mastra/mcp/src/client/internal.ts`** (or wherever `InternalMastraMCPClient` lives) — accept a `coerceSchemasTo: "zod" | "json-schema"` option; when `"zod"`, route `convertInputSchema` / `convertOutputSchema` through `zod-from-json-schema`.
2. **`@mastra/mcp/src/client/MCPClient.ts`** — pass the option through from the public constructor.
3. **`@mastra/mcp/package.json`** — add `zod-from-json-schema` as a dependency.
4. **`@mastra/mcp` test suite** — add the unit tests above and (if the package has a workerd matrix) the integration test.
5. **`@mastra/mcp` README / docs** — call out the option, why it exists, and recommend it for edge / workerd / Deno Deploy deployments.
6. **Changeset** — minor bump on `@mastra/mcp` (additive option, no behavior change at default).

`@mastra/schema-compat` is intentionally untouched. AJV stays.

---

## References

- workerd's restriction on `eval` / `new Function`: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/reference/security-model/
- AJV's use of `new Function`: https://github.com/ajv-validator/ajv/blob/v8/lib/compile/index.ts
- `zod-from-json-schema` (codegen-free JSON-Schema → Zod converter): https://www.npmjs.com/package/zod-from-json-schema
- Standard Schema spec: https://github.com/standard-schema/standard-schema

## Appendix: full call stack on failure

```
agent.stream(...)
└─ AI SDK invokes Tool.execute
   └─ CoreToolBuilder.build()'s wrapped execute
      └─ validateToolInput(parameters, args, "searchGics")
         └─ safeValidate(schema, data)
            └─ schema["~standard"].validate(data)
               └─ JsonSchemaWrapper.#validate(value)        // @mastra/schema-compat
                  └─ JsonSchemaWrapper.#getAjvValidator()
                     └─ new Ajv(...).compile(this.#schema)
                        └─ ajv/dist/compile/index.ts → makeValidate
                           └─ new Function(scope, sourceCode)  // ← workerd CSP rejects
                        └─ caught, surfaced as { issues: [{ message: "Schema validation error: Code generation from strings disallowed for this context" }] }
               └─ returned to validateToolInput
            └─ formatted: "Tool input validation failed for searchGics. ..."
      └─ returned to the LLM as the tool's error result
```

comments.body:
I've found a way to mask the sensitive information, but the tool-related errors will likely still persist in the output. I believe it would be more user-friendly if we could control this at a more fundamental level.

```TypeScript
execute: async ({ writer }) => {
  for await (const part of toAISdkStream(result, {
    from: 'agent',
    // sanitizeStreamError contains a generic string shown during general errors
    onError: sanitizeStreamError, 
  })!) {
    writer.write(part);
  }
}
```
Proposed Changes
- Current State: We are hiding sensitive data, but raw tool errors still leak through the stream.

- Ideal State: Implement a more robust way to intercept and manage tool-level errors before they reach the writer object, providing a cleaner experience for the end user.

