### Describe the Bug

Same class of issue as #14148 (Anthropic `web_search`) but on **Gemini `code_execution` via Vertex AI**. A single missing `codeExecutionResult` part in the provider response becomes an orphaned `state:"call"` tool invocation in thread memory, after which **every subsequent turn on the thread returns an empty response**.

The initial miss appears to depend on Vertex/Gemini server state — not reproducible on every call, the model does emit `executableCode` correctly but the paired `codeExecutionResult` is sometimes omitted (often alongside `finishReason: "OTHER"` / `"UNEXPECTED_TOOL_CALL"`). That upstream behavior is effectively uncontrollable. **The deterministic part is what happens next**: once the orphan lands in Mastra memory, every replay of that thread is stuck — which is where a Mastra-layer safeguard would help.

### Related

- #14148 — same symptom, Anthropic `web_search`
- #13126 — fixed parallel stream-bail for provider-executed tools (does not cover this variant)
- #14192, #14243 — closed/unmerged attempts at orphan sanitization

### Minimal Reproduction (direct Vertex call, no Mastra)

This reproduces the **second half** of the chain — the deterministic cascade from an already-orphaned history. The script sends a `contents` array where the last turn is an unresolved `code_execution` `functionCall`, exactly as Mastra persists it after Vertex omits the result. 5 trials show the model returns empty text, 5/5. Then the same history with a synthetic `functionResponse` appended — 5/5 normal output.

```js
import { GoogleAuth } from "google-auth-library"

const creds = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(process.env.GOOGLE_VERTEX_CREDENTIALS_BASE64, "base64"))
const auth = new GoogleAuth({ credentials: creds, scopes: ["https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform"] })
const { token } = await (await auth.getClient()).getAccessToken()

// History Mastra would persist after Vertex drops codeExecutionResult:
const orphanContents = [
  { role: "user", parts: [{ text: "Parse the attached spreadsheet and tell me the column names." }] },
  { role: "model", parts: [
    { text: "I'll parse it with pandas via code_execution." },
    { functionCall: {
      name: "code_execution",
      args: { code: "import pandas as pd; print(pd.read_excel('/tmp/x.xlsx').columns.tolist())" }
    }}
  ]},
  // No matching functionResponse / codeExecutionResult
]

const tools = [
  { codeExecution: {} },
  { functionDeclarations: [
    { name: "dbt_search", description: "search", parameters: { type: "object", properties: {} }},
    { name: "slack_search", description: "search", parameters: { type: "object", properties: {} }},
  ]},
]

const project = process.env.GOOGLE_VERTEX_PROJECT
const url = `https://aiplatform.googleapis.com/v1/projects/${project}/locations/global/publishers/google/models/gemini-3-flash-preview:streamGenerateContent?alt=sse`

async function run(body, label) {
  const res = await fetch(url, {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`, "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify(body),
  })
  const raw = await res.text()
  const finish = [...raw.matchAll(/"finishReason":\s*"([^"]+)"/g)].map(m => m[1])
  const textChunks = [...raw.matchAll(/"text":\s*"([^"]+)"/g)].map(m => m[1])
  const finalText = textChunks.slice(-1)[0] ?? ""
  console.log(`${label}: finishReason=${finish.join(",")} textEmpty=${finalText.trim().length===0}`)
}

const baseBody = {
  contents: orphanContents,
  tools,
  toolConfig: { functionCallingConfig: { mode: "AUTO" } },
  generationConfig: { thinkingConfig: { includeThoughts: true } },
}

// Scenario A — orphan as-is (5/5 empty response)
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) await run(baseBody, `A trial ${i + 1}`)

// Scenario B — with synthetic functionResponse paired (5/5 normal)
const withPseudo = {
  ...baseBody,
  contents: [...orphanContents, {
    role: "user",
    parts: [{ functionResponse: {
      name: "code_execution",
      response: { name: "code_execution", content: "ERROR: tool execution unavailable" },
    }}]
  }],
}
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) await run(withPseudo, `B trial ${i + 1}`)
```

Expected output (verified):

```
A trial 1..5: finishReason=STOP textEmpty=true
B trial 1..5: finishReason=STOP textEmpty=false
```

### How Mastra produces the orphan

1. Gemini emits `executableCode` for `code_execution`. In the failing case Vertex returns `finishReason: UNEXPECTED_TOOL_CALL` / `OTHER` without emitting the paired `codeExecutionResult`.
2. AI SDK (`@ai-sdk/google`) emits a `tool-call { providerExecuted: true }` chunk with no paired `tool-result` chunk.
3. Mastra persists the assistant message with `tool-invocation { state: "call", providerExecuted: true }` and no matching result.
4. On the next turn, `convert-to-google-generative-ai-messages.ts:292` serializes the stored tool call as a bare `functionCall` part. Gemini now sees an unpaired functionCall in `contents` and returns `text: ""` — for every subsequent turn on the thread.

### Expected Behavior

On stream completion, if a tool invocation is still `{ providerExecuted: true, state: "call" }` with no paired result, Mastra should either:
- Strip the orphan from memory before persisting (similar intent to closed #14192), **or**
- Synthesize a placeholder `functionResponse` (e.g. `"ERROR: tool execution unavailable"`) so the next turn has a valid call/response pair.

Empirically, the short synthetic content `"ERROR: tool execution unavailable"` yields the best behavior — across 5 trials, clean `STOP` with no retry attempts and no empty text. Longer/explanatory contents (e.g. `"Execution completed: [simulated]"` or multi-sentence "Do NOT retry" instructions) cause the model to attempt `code_execution` again, often triggering another `UNEXPECTED_TOOL_CALL`.

Since the upstream miss is apparently non-deterministic and outside Mastra's control, a safeguard in the Mastra tool-call persistence path would materially improve Mastra's resilience for any thread using Gemini builtin tools (the same pattern applies to `google_search` as well).

### Environment

- `@mastra/core`: 1.23.0
- `@ai-sdk/google-vertex`: 4.0.95
- `@ai-sdk/google`: 3.0.53
- Model: `gemini-3-flash-preview` on Vertex AI
- Node.js: v24.14.1

### Impact

- Thread-level cascade: once a single orphan is persisted, **all subsequent turns** on that thread return empty text
- No recovery without manual memory surgery (deleting the orphaned message)
- Observed across multiple independent Mastra deployments in production
- Same underlying defect class as #14148 (Anthropic web_search) — a single safeguard in the persistence path could cover both providers

### Verification checklist
- [x] I have searched existing issues (found #14148, #14192, #14243 as prior art)
- [x] I have included a minimal reproducible example

### Problem Statement

Workspace options like `skills`, `instructions`, `model`, `tools`, and `memory` all support dynamic functions that receive `requestContext`:

```ts
skills: (context) => { ... }          // ✅ dynamic
```

But `sandbox` only accepts a static instance:

```ts
// no way to resolve dynamically per request
sandbox: new LocalSandbox({
    workingDirectory: './workspace',
})
```

### Proposed Solution

Allow `sandbox` to accept a function that receives `requestContext`, matching the pattern of every other dynamic option:

```ts
const workspace = new Workspace({
    sandbox: ({ requestContext }) => {
        const role = requestContext.get('agent-role');
        return new LocalSandbox({
            workingDirectory: `/workspaces/${role}`,
        });
    },
    // ...
});
```

### Component

Agents

### Alternatives Considered

_No response_

### Example Use Case

We run a multi-role agent on a shared workspace. Each role should be scoped to a different working directory or have different execution permissions. Today we're forced to create separate `Workspace` instances per role at runtime. A dynamic `sandbox` resolver would let a single `Workspace` instance scope execution per request — different working directories, different permissions, or even different sandbox implementations.


### Additional Context

This is essentially the same request as #13133 for `filesystem` — both options suffer from the same limitation.

